---
import "../../../styles/global.css";
import SkillResourceModal from "../../../components/SkillResourceModal.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/favicon.svg`} />
		<link rel="icon" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/favicon.ico`} />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
		<meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
		<title>Handling Medication Variations</title>
	</head>
	<body class="bg-base-200 min-h-screen">
		<main class="mx-auto max-w-4xl p-6 lg:p-10">
			<div class="breadcrumbs text-sm mb-6">
				<ul>
					<li><a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}>Home</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/skills`}>Skills</a></li>
					<li>Handling Medication Variations</li>
				</ul>
			</div>

			<section class="hero bg-base-100 rounded-box shadow-xl">
				<div class="hero-content w-full flex-col items-start gap-4">
					<h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Handling Medication Variations</h1>
					<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-4">
						<a href="https://lrps.wgu.edu/provision/340289011" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-secondary resource-link">
							Open LRPS
						</a>
					</div>
					<a href="https://connect.elsevierperformancemanager.com/#/content/skills/13695/videos" class="btn btn-primary resource-link">
						Watch Video
					</a>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Summary -->
			<div class="card bg-base-100 shadow-xl mt-6">
				<div class="card-body">
					<h2 class="card-title text-2xl">Summary</h2>
					<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
						<li>Always compare MAR, provider order, and medication label three times, and verify expiration date before administration</li>
						<li>Apply the six rights of medication administration with every dose: right medication, dose, patient, route, time, and documentation</li>
						<li>If a patient refuses medication, assess the reason first and distinguish side effects from serious adverse reactions</li>
						<li>For reported allergies, withhold the medication, verify documentation, update records per policy, and notify the provider</li>
						<li>When side effects are the barrier, consider timing, food intake, or provider-approved dose/frequency adjustments</li>
						<li>If medication appearance differs, verify order carefully and consider generic substitution; hold and clarify if look-alike/sound-alike risk exists</li>
						<li>Use clinical judgment for PRN medications: assess need, timing of last dose, and expected onset/peak/duration before giving</li>
						<li>Document all administrations, refusals, or held doses with reason and provider notification details when applicable</li>
						<li>Follow up on outcomes, especially symptom relief and safety, and report inadequate effect or refusal for plan adjustment</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Transcript -->
			<div class="card bg-base-100 shadow-xl mt-6">
				<div class="card-body">
					<h2 class="card-title text-2xl">Transcript</h2>
					<p>Before administering any medication, check the medication administration record, or MAR, against the provider's order. Compare the label of the medication with the information in the MAR twice, and do a third check at the bedside. Also check the expiration date of the medication. As you proceed, observe the six rights of medication administration: right medication, right dose, right patient, right route, right time, and right documentation. To review them in detail, see the Video Skill, "Ensuring the Six Rights of Medication Administration."</p>
					<p>Patients are becoming more and more active in their own health care. They often ask questions or volunteer important information when they receive their medication. Handling these variations confidently and professionally helps keep patients safe. After performing hand hygiene and ensuring privacy, introduce yourself to the patient. Then identify her using two identifiers.</p>
					<p>Sometimes a patient will choose not to take a medication. To handle this situation, assess her reason for refusing the medication. Then intervene appropriately. For example, if the patient says she's allergic to the medication, withhold it and check her medical record for documentation. If the allergy hasn't been noted, document it as required by agency policy, and place an allergy ID band on the patient. Then notify her health care provider, and request that the medication order be amended.</p>
					<p>It's not uncommon for a patient to refuse a medication because he anticipates having unpleasant or inconvenient side effects. Assess the patient to make sure he's describing side effects and not a life-threatening adverse reaction. Then try to eliminate or reduce the side effects by suggesting that he take the medication with food or at a different time. Explain your reasoning, and emphasize his need for the medication. Since most side effects are dose dependent, you can also ask the prescriber to reduce the dosage if possible or to order smaller doses at more frequent intervals.</p>
					<p>A patient may question you if the medication you give her has a different color or shape than she's used to. After verifying the prescriber's order, explain to her that a generic drug might have been substituted for a brand-name drug, and that even generic drugs often differ in appearance from one another, depending on the manufacturer. Be aware, though, that some medications have look-alike and sound-alike names that often contribute to drug errors, even among experienced health care professionals. It may be appropriate to withhold the drug until you can verify the order with the pharmacy.</p>
					<p>A patient may make an inappropriate request for a medication. If you have a PRN order for a medication, use your clinical judgment. Don't give medications automatically, without assessing the patient's need. Become familiar with the onset and duration of action for each medication you administer, and be aware of how long it'll take to reach its peak and begin to plateau.</p>
					<p>If a patient requests a PRN analgesic, first assess the patient's pain. Then check the MAR to see what time his previous dose was given. This patient's MAR shows that Percocet is to be given every 4 hours PRN, and that the last dose was given at nine A.M. It's now twenty after one. It is time for the next dose if the patient is having pain. Identify the patient with two identifiers. Help him sit up, and watch him take the medication. Then help him back to a comfortable position. Afterward, document the dose in the patient's MAR.</p>
					<p>If a medication was refused, document that it was not given, the reason for refusal, and the time at which the health care provider was notified. If a medication is held, document the time and reason for holding the medication, and notify the health care provider if necessary.</p>
					<p>As part of your follow-up care, monitor the patient's response to determine whether the interventions have relieved his pain. Notify the health care provider if the patient refuses the medication or if it has inadequate effects. The dosage, the drug, or its formulation may need to be changed.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<SkillResourceModal />
		</main>
	</body>
</html>
